WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.963
Model binding converts request data into
page model properties,

2
00:00:07.043 --> 00:00:11.038
handler parameters, and input objects.

3
00:00:11.118 --> 00:00:18.521
That convenience is one of the reasons
Razor Pages can be pleasant to build.

4
00:00:18.601 --> 00:00:24.829
It also creates a boundary that must be
designed deliberately.

5
00:00:24.909 --> 00:00:30.784
A value that appears in a typed property
has been converted;

6
00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:33.919
it has not become trustworthy.

7
00:00:33.999 --> 00:00:41.755
Bound data should be treated as untrusted
until the server validates it for the

8
00:00:41.835 --> 00:00:43.010
operation.

9
00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:50.140
Validation should include required fields,
types, lengths, formats,

10
00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:58.328
ranges, enumerated values, relationships,
workflow state, and business rules.

11
00:00:58.408 --> 00:01:02.521
A valid shape is not always a valid
action,

12
00:01:02.601 --> 00:01:09.181
and a valid action may depend on the
current user and target record.

13
00:01:09.261 --> 00:01:16.194
Input models and view models help define
the intended input contract.

14
00:01:16.274 --> 00:01:22.736
A page that updates a display name should
not accept account role,

15
00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:29.749
approval state, tenant identifier,
internal notes, or audit fields.

16
00:01:29.829 --> 00:01:37.702
Narrow models make it easier for reviewers
to see what the user is allowed to send

17
00:01:37.782 --> 00:01:40.250
and what the server owns.

18
00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:48.790
Avoid binding directly to broad domain
entities when doing so could allow accidental

19
00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.395
modification of sensitive fields.

20
00:01:52.475 --> 00:02:00.348
Domain objects often contain more data
than a page should accept from a request.

21
00:02:00.428 --> 00:02:08.066
They may include ownership, status,
pricing, configuration, audit values,

22
00:02:08.146 --> 00:02:10.731
or administrative flags.

23
00:02:10.811 --> 00:02:18.214
Those fields should usually be set by
server logic, not by general binding.

24
00:02:18.294 --> 00:02:25.580
Client-side validation improves usability
by catching mistakes early,

25
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:30.477
but it does not replace server-side
validation.

26
00:02:30.557 --> 00:02:38.313
The server must enforce the rules it
depends on because clients can be changed,

27
00:02:38.393 --> 00:02:41.918
disabled, scripted, or out of date.

28
00:02:41.998 --> 00:02:50.458
A state-changing handler should check
model validity and operation-specific rules

29
00:02:50.538 --> 00:02:52.888
before making changes.

30
00:02:52.968 --> 00:02:58.726
Safe update patterns assign intended
fields explicitly.

31
00:02:58.806 --> 00:03:06.209
Instead of allowing a bound object to
overwrite a stored record wholesale,

32
00:03:06.289 --> 00:03:13.457
load the trusted record, verify access,
validate the requested change,

33
00:03:13.537 --> 00:03:19.177
and assign only the fields the page is
designed to update.

34
00:03:19.257 --> 00:03:26.660
That pattern reduces over-posting and
makes future review more reliable.
